 # Session and API Key Validators/Middleware TODO

 ## Session validation

 - [ ] Define the session model and required fields (ID, user ID, expiration, revocation status).
 - [ ] Add validation for missing, malformed, expired, and revoked session tokens.
 - [ ] Verify session tokens securely and avoid trusting client-provided user IDs.
 - [ ] Load the session and authenticated user into the request context.
 - [ ] Add configurable session expiration and refresh behavior.
 - [ ] Implement logout/revocation handling and session cleanup.

 ## API key validation

 - [ ] Define the API key format, storage model, scopes, status, and expiration rules.
 - [ ] Validate missing, malformed, expired, revoked, and disabled API keys.
 - [ ] Hash API keys at rest and compare them using a constant-time method.
 - [ ] Load key metadata and allowed scopes into the request context.
 - [ ] Support key rotation and revocation without exposing secret values.

 ## Middleware

 - [ ] Create authentication middleware that accepts only the intended credential locations.
 - [ ] Add authorization middleware for required roles/scopes per route.
 - [ ] Define precedence and conflict behavior when both a session and API key are supplied.
 - [ ] Return consistent `401` and `403` responses without leaking credential details.
 - [ ] Add rate limiting and abuse protection for authentication failures.
 - [ ] Ensure middleware handles errors, cancellation, and missing context values safely.

 ## Testing and operations

 - [ ] Add unit tests for each validator and edge case.
 - [ ] Add integration tests covering protected routes and credential combinations.
 - [ ] Test token/key rotation, revocation, expiration, and clock boundaries.
 - [ ] Add structured audit logging without logging tokens or API key secrets.
 - [ ] Document configuration, route usage, and security assumptions.
